WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.160
If only someone had given Nick Fury the same
kind of warning Santa Claus once gave Ralphie

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:05.660
Parker.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:07.760
"You’ll shoot your eye out, kid."

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:14.560
Ever since Samuel L. Jackson revealed in January
2018 that Captain Marvel would feature Fury

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:18.880
without his signature eyepatch, fans everywhere
have been wondering the same thing: would

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.680
the newest MCU entry explain how he actually
lost his eye?

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:26.860
Jackson and the whole Captain Marvel team,
including star Brie Larson and co-directors

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:31.330
Anna Boden and Ryan Fleck, have kept quiet
about that particular element of the film,

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:32.660
but now we know the truth.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:37.560
Captain Marvel does indeed see Fury sustain
the injury that ultimately costs him his eye.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:42.620
In doing so, the film also answers a mystery
that's gone unsolved for 11 years, since the

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.340
debut of Iron Man and the beginning of the
MCU as we know it.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.300
Spoilers ahead, obviously.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:52.020
So, here's what happens with Fury and his
infamous eye in Captain Marvel: Carol Danvers'

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:57.249
best friend and fellow Air Force pilot Maria
Rambeau boards a ship with Fury after evading

00:00:57.249 --> 00:01:00.660
the Kree Starforce, who are actually the main
bad guys in the film.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:05.560
Unbeknownst to Maria and Fury, Goose, the
fuzzy orange cat Fury often cuddled with and

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.880
cooed at throughout the duration of Captain
Marvel is in tow as well.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:13.700
It’s worth noting here that Goose isn't
actually a cat: he's a Flerken, an alien creature

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.480
who only takes a feline appearance.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:20.260
And it’s Goose who plays a big part in getting
Fury and company to safety during the film’s

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:20.760
final act.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.720
When he realizes the cat has made his way
onto his ship, Fury scoops Goose up and thanks

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:29.750
him for helping them on their mission to save
Earth, protecting the shape-shifting Skrulls,

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.140
and retrieving the Tesseract.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:36.100
As Maria and Fury attempt to escape the Kree,
Goose opens his mouth and extends several

00:01:36.109 --> 00:01:39.939
octopus-like arms to grab the Tesseract and
swallow it whole.

00:01:39.939 --> 00:01:45.030
Luckily, Goose has a pocket dimension in which
he's able to "store entire universes" and

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:46.060
access them at will.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:50.399
Naturally, Fury believes that Goose would
never hurt him, since he’s on their side.

00:01:50.399 --> 00:01:55.240
As Fury delivers baby-talk to Goose, the cat
takes a swipe at his face, leaving several

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.740
scratch marks across Fury's eyebrow and lid.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.060
Fury and Maria assume that the scratch is
nothing major, but the injury only gets worse

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.060
as the film wraps up.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:07.380
By the very end of the movie, we see Fury
wearing a metallic-looking cover over the

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:08.040
injured eye which is now missing entirely.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:13.060
Agent Phil Coulson steps into Fury's office,
hands him a case full of fake eyes, and jokes

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:14.780
that he has a big decision to make.

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:20.360
He then asks if it's true that the Kree burned
Fury's eye out, a rumor Fury neither confirms

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:24.340
nor denies, signaling that he's learned to
stay cautious of everyone around him after

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:28.280
his life-changing adventures with Captain
Marvel, the Kree, and the Skrulls.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:32.720
The injury Fury suffers in Captain Marvel
also connects nicely to the only explicit

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:35.760
statement Fury has given about his eye in
the MCU thus far.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:39.760
Fans have wondered what happened to Fury's
eye since we first met him in Iron Man, but

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.860
he's made only ever one direct mention of
it:

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:45.140
"Last time I trusted someone, I lost an eye."

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:51.660
Though the someone Fury trusted is really
a something, that mystery is finally solved:

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:56.200
Fury trusted Goose to be a friendly companion,
thanked him for his role in completing the

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:00.849
mission, and eventually needs to wear an eyepatch
for the rest of his life because of it.

00:03:00.849 --> 00:03:06.209
A cat-like alien probably isn't the untrustworthy
entity fans were thinking of when Fury first

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:10.300
spoke about losing his eye, but it's now officially
canon in the MCU.

